        "id": 1006780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T17:03:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My interpretation of what your FIXME is suggesting is that you want SubscriptionManager#canManageSubscription to return true for active subscriptions based on the carrier privilege rules of those subscriptions (e.g. hasCarrierPrivileges). But those rules are two different sets of rules. canManageSubscription is based on eSIM metadata external to a profile; hasCarrierPrivileges is based on the ARA/ARF data within a profile. For better or worse we\u0027ve defined two different sets of semantics for those two rules - one related to management of the subscription at a meta level, vs. one related to carrier operations within the scope of that subscription.",
      "parentUuid": "cc28839e_85fbe967",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 832,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 833,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "933fd70634ab44156c6f82522afe88d08221cf63",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "da4deebf_bbdc489b",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/SubscriptionInfoUpdater.java",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 833,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T17:55:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, I spoke to jpd@ offline since there\u0027s definitely some confusion here. The root of the problem is that carrierPrivilegeRules and ARA/ARF rules grant distinct types of permission, so we need to draw a distinction between what they do:\n1) The ARA/ARF rules grant the ability to add and delete subscriptions (profiles) on an eSIM.\n2) The carrier privilege rules grant the ability to modify the contents of those profiles.\n\nSince those ARE distinct concepts, and since SM.canManageSubscription() only concerns itself with the former (and we see no straightforward alternative that allows such a mechanism to work without an eSIM), I propose the following:\n\n1) We change the name of canManageSubscription() to canManageProfile() or canManageEmbeddedProfile() etc since it operates on the profile, not the subscription. (It\u0027s also documented to only work on \"embedded\" subscriptions).\n\n2) We need some clear definable boundaries between what carrier privilege grants vs what profile management ability grants. Right now, we\u0027re saying that either is sufficient to allow subscriptions to be grouped: that may or may not should be true... I\u0027m starting to think we shouldn\u0027t let inactive embedded subscriptions be added to groups either and that it should be strictly within the realm of carrier privilege.\n\n3) We also need to define what system privileged apps can do in terms of profile management since they will not have carrier privilege or ARA rules and yet probably canManageEmbeddedProfile given some other specific permission? Thinking out loud, I bet that permission already exists?",
      "parentUuid": "ddfed6f1_0b4e9ff9",
